    "knowledge_point": "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
    "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$",
